<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Celebration · 1884 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Aogeba Tōtoshi</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ja">仰げば尊し</bdi> / <bdi lang="ja-Latn">Aogeba Tōtoshi</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song sung at Japanese school graduations since the 1880s, in which pupils honour their teachers as they say goodbye.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>not known (credited to Isawa Shūji, or jointly to Ōtsuki Fumihiko, Satomi Tadashi and Kabe Iwao)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>not known (credited to Isawa Shūji; the tune is that of &quot;Song for the Close of School&quot;, music by &quot;H. N. D.&quot;, 1871)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Japanese (the schools of the Meiji era)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Japan</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first published 1884</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (published 1884 in the Ministry of Education's songbook for primary schools; every author it is credited to died long since)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aogeba-totoshi">Aogeba Tōtoshi</a>&quot; is a song sung at graduation ceremonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, usually in the key of D or E-flat major, in 6/8 time. Its first known appearance was in 1884, when the Meiji-era educator Isawa Shūji (1851–1917) added it to the Ministry of Education's published collection of songs for primary-school pupils. Who wrote it has long been uncertain. Some insist that Isawa wrote both the words and the music; the words are also said to have been written together by Ōtsuki Fumihiko (1847–1928), Satomi Tadashi (1824–1886) and Kabe Iwao. The melody was often said to come from a traditional Scottish folk song of uncertain origin, as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hotaru-no-hikari">Hotaru no Hikari</a>&quot; borrowed the tune of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auld-lang-syne">Auld Lang Syne</a>&quot;.</p><p>In January 2011 Masato Sakurai, professor emeritus of Hitotsubashi University, announced that he believed he had found the song's origin in an English-language school music book, The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Echo, published in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1871. Its &quot;Song for the Close of School&quot;, with words by T. H. Brosnan and music by &quot;H. N. D.&quot;, is, according to Sakurai, exactly the same as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aogeba-totoshi">Aogeba Tōtoshi</a>&quot;. The American song is no longer known in the United States.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, some felt that the song's worshipful attitude towards teachers did not suit a democracy. Opposition to it became part of a wider reaction against the old order during the student protests of the 1960s, and schools hesitated to play it at graduations for fear of protest. When the protests died down, older notions continued to fade, and the song, whose grammar and vocabulary were archaic even in the 1880s, was largely dropped by public schools, especially primary schools, in favour of newer songs such as &quot;Tabidachi no Hi ni&quot;, Kaientai's &quot;Okuru Kotoba&quot; and Naotarō Moriyama's &quot;Sakura&quot;. Schools that kept it often left out the second stanza, whose call to rise in the world and make a name for oneself seemed out of step with a changing society. After the war the children's author Tamao Fujita published a version with modernised words, but parents did not take to it, because it did not bring tears the way the original did.</p><p>In 2007 the Agency for Cultural Affairs and the National Congress of Parents and Teachers Associations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> chose &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aogeba-totoshi">Aogeba Tōtoshi</a>&quot; for the Nihon no Uta Hyakusen, a selection of 100 songs of Japan. As the list is ordered by the Japanese syllabary, it stands first.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A farewell of pupils to their teachers on the day they leave school. The singers look up with reverence to the kindness of their teachers and marvel at how fast the years of schooling have gone; they ask their schoolfellows not to forget one another after they part, urge themselves to rise in the world and make a name, and remember the window where they studied morning and evening by the light of fireflies and of snow, an old image of hard study. Every stanza closes on the moment of parting and farewell. The mood is solemn and tearful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Japanese</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">あふげばたふとし。わが師の恩。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">教の庭にも。はやいくとせ。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">おもへばいと疾し。このとし月。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">今こそわかれめ。いざゝらば。</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">互にむつみし。日ごろの恩。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">わかるゝ後にも。やよわするな。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">身をたて名をあげ。やよはげめよ。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">いまこそわかれめ。いざゝらば。</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">朝ゆふなれにし。まなびの窓。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">ほたるのともし火。つむ白雪。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">わするゝまぞなき。ゆくとし月。</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ja">今こそわかれめ。いざゝらば。</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Shōgaku shōkashū, part 3 (Ministry of Education, 1884), as transcribed on Japanese Wikisource. Text from <a href="https://ja.wikisource.org/wiki/%E4%BB%B0%E3%81%92%E3%81%B0%E5%B0%8A%E3%81%97" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
